00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:30.240
When you go for a walk and feel, perhaps,
the soft “squish” of nature underneath

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:38.699
your feet, you are in fact walking along the
home of plants and fungi and microbes.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:45.840
The soil itself might just seem like dirt,
common, ubiquitous, mundane.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.050
But dirt is essential.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.929
It is where everything you eat comes from.

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:55.550
It recycles waste.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.210
It cleans water.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:03.969
It makes your life better in millions of tiny
ways you never notice.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:13.840
And also...it is mysterious: housing a complex
network of reactions between plants and microbes.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.680
We can’t see it with our eyes, but it’s
like an economy built on chemical signals

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:26.020
and nutrients and competition that take place
in the microcosmos, but that shape the macro

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.200
world as well.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.970
Now, microbes require water to thrive, which
can be challenging in soil.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.820
On this channel, we’ve seen many examples
of the protective cysts that many microbes

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:45.390
form when the habitat around them dries out,
like these ciliates that will remain in this

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:50.270
state until the conditions become right for
them to emerge again.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.460
But protective cysts are only a small part
of the story.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:02.280
Soil is itself a mix of organic material,
minerals, water, and air.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.280
And that fraction of water doesn’t just
sustain the plant life we see above ground,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:16.860
it provides a home to many, many microorganisms
that remain hidden below.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.530
Soil is also not some mound of dirt that would
be uniform but for the interruption of animals

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.170
and plant roots.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.040
That world underneath our feet is made up
of layers and zones.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.840
For example, there’s bulk soil, the area
that is untouched by plant roots.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:40.959
Bulk soil is home to microbes, but at the
turn of the 20th century, a German scientist

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:47.909
named Lorenz Hiltner noticed that the closer
the soil was to a plant’s root, the higher

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:51.580
bacterial activity he would observe.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:59.540
And from this work, he defined not just a
new term, but a whole new world: the world

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.150
of the rhizosphere.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:09.920
When Hiltner defined the rhizosphere in 1904,
he described it as the area of the soil that

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.040
most immediately surrounded a plant root.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.939
But in time, this definition has been expanded
to describe not just the soil around the roots,

00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:23.999
but also the parts of the root that get overtaken
by microbes.

00:03:23.999 --> 00:03:27.519
But the rhizosphere isn’t just a location.

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.999
It’s a whole set of interactions between
plants and microbes.

00:03:32.999 --> 00:03:38.849
And with the complex ecological relationship
that builds, the soil of the rhizosphere can

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:45.730
actually look quite different from bulk soil,
from the types of nutrients to its pH.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:51.340
But different plants have different needs,
and so the rhizosphere is a dynamic and varied

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.730
thing that changes according to the microbial
communities that inhabit it and the complicated

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.280
chemical language they share.

00:04:00.889 --> 00:04:04.240
In the big picture, roots provide structure
to plants.

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:10.059
But they also play an important role in the
rhizosphere, sending out chemical signals

00:04:10.059 --> 00:04:12.379
that attract microbes.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:18.900
These chemicals can include everything from
free oxygen to water to enzymes to mucilage

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.240
to proteins to all sorts of other things.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.530
Collectively, these compounds are known as
the “root exudate.” and they might be

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:33.380
part of a plant’s waste that gets excreted
through the root and into the surroundings.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.460
Or they might be released either actively
or passively from root cells.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:43.840
Very broadly, the role of root exudate is
to attract microbes.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.680
In some terms, you could consider it an invitation,
but it’s an invitation that comes with a

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.950
cost for the plant.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:59.349
Some of those exudates are nutrients the plant
themselves could use, so why spend them on

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:00.780
an invitation?

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.270
What can the microbes bring in exchange?

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:10.110
Well, for one, microbes help plants access
nutrients they might have trouble getting

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.870
on their own.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:17.880
Above ground, plants can access carbon dioxide,
water, oxygen, and light.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.460
And below ground, they get structure and many
of the additional mineral elements they need

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.770
to survive.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:30.159
But some elements, like nitrogen, are pretty
difficult for plants to get.

00:05:30.159 --> 00:05:37.860
Nitrogen is essential for life, and it is
also plentiful, but it is so difficult to

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:46.020
convert Nitrogen into the forms life needs
and plants aren’t able to do that.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:53.210
Instead, they rely on the bacteria of the
rhizosphere to take Nitrogen and convert it

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:57.410
into ammonia and other compounds that they
can actually use.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:05.300
Some plants, like legumes, even form nodules
on their roots to house those bacteria, creating

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.639
an even tighter symbiotic relationship.

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:14.090
Now, whenever you invite someone over, there’s
a little bit of a risk to that, especially

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:19.230
when, you know, sometimes people misunderstand
the nature of the invitation.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:24.560
Releasing compounds that attract bacteria
is all well and good until the pathogens also

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.419
catch wind of what’s going on.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:32.020
Sometimes these pathogens make it to the plant
and are able to infect it.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:37.580
But then other times, the non-pathogenic bacteria
can shield the plant, creating protective

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.930
biofilms or releasing toxic compounds that
target the pathogens.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:47.000
Like your good friends that help you kick
that guy out, who I don’t think you even

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.350
invited.

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:55.000
But collecting such an active bacterial community
is also a great way to attract predators.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.469
Protozoa and nematodes alike can be found
in the rhizosphere thanks to the buffet of

00:06:59.469 --> 00:07:02.199
of bacteria that await them.

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:06.830
And yet this is a risk that plays somewhat
into the plant’s interests as well: as these

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:13.500
predators graze on the bacteria, they excrete
nitrogenous waste that can continue to contribute

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.919
to plant growth.

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:22.189
(Or, in some cases, the predator dies, and
they become the source of nutrients.)

00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:28.610
The composition of these microbial communities
is shaped by the compounds released by the

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.310
plant’s roots.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.590
Scientists are studying the ways these specific
molecules in the root exudate can influence

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.590
the presence of specific bacteria and fungi.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:44.949
As we learn more about the rhizosphere, we
may come to understand not only how these

00:07:44.949 --> 00:07:52.380
root exudates influence the ecology of microbes
and plants alike, but how we can better interact

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:54.860
with it when growing our own crops.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:08:00.080
These chemical signals and the interactions
they shape form a dialogue between different

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.139
parts of the living world.

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:08.210
They speak a language whose basic vocabulary
we may understand, but whose full syntax is

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:14.580
complex and wonderful and mysterious.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:21.180
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

